WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What does a battery showing seventy-five percent tell you?

00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:08.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are exploring percent as a way of describing
relative size.

00:00:08.695 --> 00:00:15.370 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will use one whole divided into one hundred equal parts
and compare familiar percentages on the same scale.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.438 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to explain percentages as parts of a
whole divided into 100 equal parts.

00:00:23.088 --> 00:00:24.838 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:27.900 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain what the percent symbol means.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.282 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can represent a familiar percentage using a hundred-part
model.

00:00:32.502 --> 00:00:36.069 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can compare percentages by thinking about the same whole.

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:38.530 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Percent means per hundred.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.611 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One hundred percent is one complete whole.

00:00:41.831 --> 00:00:44.255 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Fifty percent is half of that whole.

00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:50.203 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always identify the whole: a percentage describes a share of
it, rather than a count by itself.

00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:57.300 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A battery at seventy-five percent represents seventy-five of
one hundred equal parts of full capacity.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.876 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is more than half full but less than completely full.

00:01:01.526 --> 00:01:06.615 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The percentage bar shows the same relative share: three
quarters of its complete length.

00:01:06.835 --> 00:01:11.251 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The full bar always stands for the same full battery in this
comparison.

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:15.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare forty percent and sixty percent of the same whole.

00:01:15.757 --> 00:01:20.893 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine forty shaded cells and sixty shaded cells on equal
hundred-grids.

00:01:21.543 --> 00:01:27.186 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sixty percent is the greater share because sixty of the
same-sized parts is more than forty.

00:01:27.406 --> 00:01:30.509 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both shares use a common scale of one hundred.

00:01:30.992 --> 00:01:35.398 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which is closer to a complete whole: ninety percent or
twenty-five percent?

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:43.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ninety percent is only ten of the hundred parts below the
whole.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.115 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twenty-five percent is seventy-five parts below it.

00:01:47.335 --> 00:01:49.397 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ninety percent is closer.

00:01:50.047 --> 00:01:52.919 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Percent means out of one hundred equal parts.

00:01:53.139 --> 00:02:00.702 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Identify the whole, use familiar benchmarks such as fifty
and one hundred percent, and compare relative shares on the
same scale.
